{"name":"Edge of the Galaxy","hex":"#303d3c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"edge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The boundary line of a surface.","A one-dimensional face of a polytope. In particular, the joining line between two vertices of a polygon; the place where two faces of a polyhedron meet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move an object slowly and carefully in a particular direction.","To move slowly and carefully in a particular direction."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/edge"},{"term":"galaxy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The Milky Way; the apparent band of concentrated stars which appears in the night sky over earth. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the collections of many millions or billions of stars, galactic dust, black holes, etc. existing as independent and coherent systems, of which there are billions in the known universe."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with galaxies.","To gather together into a luminous whole."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/galaxy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Servants of the Empire","blocks":[{"text":"Servants of the Empire is a young reader science fiction novel series by Jason Fry. Set in the Star Wars universe, the novels are affiliated with the television series Star Wars Rebels and follow the Rebels character Zare Leonis."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/64/Servants-Edge_of_the_Galaxy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servants_of_the_Empire"},{"title":"Galaxy","blocks":[{"text":"A galaxy is a physical system of stars, planetary systems, stellar remnants, substellar objects, interstellar gas, dust, and dark matter bound together by gravity. The word is derived from the Greek galaxias (γαλαξίας), meaning 'milky', a reference to the Milky Way galaxy that contains the Solar System. Galaxies, averaging an estimated 100 million stars, range in size from dwarfs with less than a thousand stars to the largest galaxies known—supergiants with one hundred trillion stars, each orbiting its galaxy's center of mass. Most of the mass in a typical galaxy is in the form of dark matter, with only a few percent of that mass visible in the form of stars and nebulae. Supermassive black holes are a common feature at the centers of galaxies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg/330px-NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galaxy"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Galaxy cluster SDSS J1152+3313. SDSS stands for Sloan Digital Sky Survey, J for Julian epoch, and 1152+3313 for right ascension and declination respectively.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProbing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Herschel-Galaxy.png/500px-Herschel-Galaxy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Herschel-Galaxy.png/500px-Herschel-